@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/climate#> .
@prefix climate: <http://example.org/orexis/climate#> .
@prefix orexis:    <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix owl:       <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:      <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:       <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sosa:      <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .

climate: a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "climate" ;
    rdfs:comment """The air a bed sits in: what moves it, and what moving it costs elsewhere.

This domain's SECOND vocabulary, beside `water:`, and the sovereign asked whether the two should
be one. They are not, and the line is a good one to know. WATER is a traded good: a litre is
bought, priced and owed, and the market's valuation terms hang off it — merging it here would
drag clearing into a vocabulary about warmth. CLIMATE is nobody's good; it is a condition, moved
by levers an agent owns outright. What the two share is a bed, and a bed is the WORLD's.

Two levers live here and they are opposite in an instructive way. A HEATER moves the air one
way, always, and needs to know nothing but that it is on. A VENT moves the air toward whatever
is outside — which may be warmer or colder, so the same act heats on one afternoon and chills on
another, and the rule must read the world to say which. That is the first effect in this
repository whose declared outcome depends on a fact no lever of the agent's can move.

Both are UN-TAKEN: nothing contributes either action, so they are planned and never executed, as
hanoi's Move is. A taker would size the run from the readings in hand, as an actuator sizes a
dose, and there is no such device here yet.""" .

#################  The heater  #################

#  NOT a `sosa:Actuator`: actuation's shapes hold every actuator to being a valve — what it
#  actuates, what it draws from, its cap in millilitres — and a heater has none of those. It
#  is a lever of its own kind, wired by this package's own two predicates.
:Heater a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "heater" ;
    rdfs:comment "A lever that warms what it is pointed at. A world states which subject and which property, because a heater over a bed and a heater in a room warm different things and the action must join through the world rather than assume." .

:hasHeater a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :Heater ;
    rdfs:label "has heater" ;
    rdfs:comment "The heaters an agent may switch on — its half of the wiring, as `actuation:hasActuator` is for a valve." .

:warms a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Heater ; rdfs:range sosa:FeatureOfInterest ;
    rdfs:label "warms" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this heater is pointed at: the bed whose air it moves. The other half of the wiring, and what makes the action available to whoever acts for that subject." .

:warmsProperty a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Heater ; rdfs:range sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:label "warms property" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHICH property this heater moves, stated by the world rather than assumed by the action: a heater warms air here, and a rule that named the property itself would be a package naming a domain's word." .

#################  The knob  #################

:driesTheSoil a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Heater ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "dries the soil" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether running this heater also dries what it warms — evaporation, as a world fact. It is the knob this domain exists for: OFF, warming touches nothing the watering want reads and the two halves of `the bed is comfortable` are independent; ON, the heater's effect writes a reading the dosing rule reads, and a plan that warmed after watering has undone its own work. The two regimes are the same world with one triple changed, which is what makes the comparison honest." .

:driesProperty a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Heater ; rdfs:range sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:label "dries property" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which property drying moves, where `climate:driesTheSoil` is true — the soil's moisture here, stated by the world for the same reason `warmsProperty` is." .

#################  The vent  #################
#
#  A window, and the first lever whose effect the agent cannot predict from its own wiring
#  alone. What opening it does to the bed depends on what is on the other side: open onto a
#  warm afternoon and the air rises, open onto a cold night and it falls. The world says which
#  subject is on the other side; the rule reads that subject's reading and works out which of
#  the bed's own bands the bed would land in.

:Vent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "vent" ;
    rdfs:comment "A window, a louvre, a fan hatch: a lever that lets the outside in. What it does to the subject is the outside's to determine, which is why it states what it opens onto." .

:hasVent a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range :Vent ;
    rdfs:label "has vent" ;
    rdfs:comment "The vents an agent may open — its half of the wiring, as `climate:hasHeater` is for a heater." .

:ventilates a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Vent ; rdfs:range sosa:FeatureOfInterest ;
    rdfs:label "ventilates" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this vent opens INTO: the bed whose air it moves." .

:ventilatesProperty a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Vent ; rdfs:range sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ventilates property" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHICH property opening it moves, stated by the world for the same reason a heater states one: a rule naming the air temperature itself would be a package spelling a domain's word." .

:opensOnto a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Vent ; rdfs:range sosa:FeatureOfInterest ;
    rdfs:label "opens onto" ;
    rdfs:comment "What is on the OTHER side — the outside air, a corridor, a second bed. Its reading is what the venting rule reads to say where the subject would land, and it is a fact no lever of this agent moves, which is exactly why the rule may read it as a NUMBER: a plan cannot change it, so it is the same number all the way down a cone. What a plan CAN change is read as the band it is (#579)." .
